WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.224 --> 00:00:02.438
- You can actually make
more money being smart

00:00:02.438 --> 00:00:04.645
than you can being strong or fast.

00:00:04.645 --> 00:00:07.368
Running your own race
demands trusting yourself

00:00:07.368 --> 00:00:08.639
even when others don't.

00:00:08.639 --> 00:00:11.389
This is not a lifestyle business.

00:00:12.294 --> 00:00:13.785
And if you want to make
it a lifestyle business,

00:00:13.785 --> 00:00:15.841
this is the wrong business to be in.

00:00:15.841 --> 00:00:18.087
- He's the founder, chairman, and CEO

00:00:18.087 --> 00:00:20.602
of Vista Equity Partners, a firm with over

00:00:20.602 --> 00:00:23.812
$26 billion under asset control.

00:00:23.812 --> 00:00:26.213
He's ranked by Forbes as the 268th

00:00:26.213 --> 00:00:27.927
richest person in America.

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:31.285
He has an estimated net worth
of over three billion dollars.

00:00:31.285 --> 00:00:33.215
He's Robert F. Smith, and here is my take

00:00:33.215 --> 00:00:35.134
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:35.134 --> 00:00:36.609
Rule number two is my personal favorite,

00:00:36.609 --> 00:00:38.327
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:38.327 --> 00:00:40.059
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:40.059 --> 00:00:41.927
Also, as Robert is talking,
if he says something

00:00:41.927 --> 00:00:43.987
that really, really is meaningful to you,

00:00:43.987 --> 00:00:45.643
please leave it in the comments below.

00:00:45.643 --> 00:00:46.918
Put quotes around it so other people

00:00:46.918 --> 00:00:49.511
can be inspired as well, enjoy.

00:00:49.511 --> 00:00:52.386
(whooshing)

00:00:52.386 --> 00:00:54.969
(upbeat music)

00:01:02.303 --> 00:01:05.553
- Intelligence can create huge profits.

00:01:07.747 --> 00:01:10.608
And in fact, you can
actually make more money

00:01:10.608 --> 00:01:14.441
being smart than you can
being strong or fast.

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:16.408
Okay?

00:01:17.931 --> 00:01:22.820
And so the importance of
developing intellectual property

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:23.653
cannot be

00:01:25.629 --> 00:01:26.879
underestimated.

00:01:28.354 --> 00:01:30.556
'Cause I want you to
think about something.

00:01:30.556 --> 00:01:32.730
Intellectual property, no one else

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.531
can take that from you.

00:01:34.531 --> 00:01:36.766
Point number one, if you manage it right.

00:01:36.766 --> 00:01:39.330
Point number two, it
is constantly evolving,

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:42.609
and it actually takes no capital.

00:01:42.609 --> 00:01:44.229
If I were to go into real estate business,

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:46.742
it takes me a whole lot of
money to go buy a building

00:01:46.742 --> 00:01:48.171
and get in real estate business.

00:01:48.171 --> 00:01:49.995
Intellectual property, I
just have to think through

00:01:49.995 --> 00:01:53.060
how it actually works,
and then develop it.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.536
And that's what really
launched me on the path

00:01:55.536 --> 00:01:59.590
to saying, you know what,
as good as I thought I was

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:01.665
playing football and all
those wonderful things,

00:02:01.665 --> 00:02:05.848
and my mom was a wonderful
governor to my ambition.

00:02:05.848 --> 00:02:09.137
She said you're a far better
scholar than you are athlete,

00:02:09.137 --> 00:02:12.720
which kind of hurt my
feelings at the time.

00:02:14.014 --> 00:02:15.611
But she was absolutely right,

00:02:15.611 --> 00:02:17.784
and I started to turn my
attention to what I call

00:02:17.784 --> 00:02:19.569
developing my mind.

00:02:19.569 --> 00:02:22.378
Running your own race
demands trusting yourself

00:02:22.378 --> 00:02:24.068
even when others don't.

00:02:24.068 --> 00:02:26.670
Because guess what, there
are a lot of people,

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:30.018
good people, people who
love you, who trust you,

00:02:30.018 --> 00:02:32.143
and who you love and trust,

00:02:32.143 --> 00:02:34.780
who want the best for you.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:36.780
And you'll come to them
with some ideas about

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.640
what you want to do and
how you want to do it,

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.557
and they're going to say that's crazy.

00:02:43.001 --> 00:02:45.926
Just like when I left
my first engineering job

00:02:45.926 --> 00:02:47.839
at Kraft and was telling
my grandfather I was

00:02:47.839 --> 00:02:49.136
going back to business school, and he said

00:02:49.136 --> 00:02:51.906
that's a great paying job, that's crazy.

00:02:51.906 --> 00:02:54.344
And when I finished business
school and decided to join

00:02:54.344 --> 00:02:56.958
the tumultuous world
of investment banking,

00:02:56.958 --> 00:02:59.819
my friends and my family
spoke up with concerns

00:02:59.819 --> 00:03:01.152
about my sanity.

00:03:01.989 --> 00:03:05.522
And when I left Goldman Sachs
right after we'd gone public

00:03:05.522 --> 00:03:08.481
to set up a private
equity firm called Vista,

00:03:08.481 --> 00:03:10.558
my mentors and colleagues at Goldman Sachs

00:03:10.558 --> 00:03:12.533
thought I was crazy.

00:03:12.533 --> 00:03:14.613
And to top it all off, when
I told 'em I'm going to start

00:03:14.613 --> 00:03:17.628
a firm, we're going to
focus on enterprise software

00:03:17.628 --> 00:03:20.459
and not be diversified like
all other private equity firms,

00:03:20.459 --> 00:03:22.362
and I was going to hire
a team of smart, young,

00:03:22.362 --> 00:03:25.227
talented people who'd
never done this before,

00:03:25.227 --> 00:03:28.361
everyone knew I was certifiable.

00:03:28.361 --> 00:03:30.367
And I did this, of course,
in the spring of 2000,

00:03:30.367 --> 00:03:33.789
about two months before the
entire tech bubble crashed.

00:03:33.789 --> 00:03:37.455
Well, all I can tell you
is I've never been mad

00:03:37.455 --> 00:03:40.541
at these folks, in fact, I'm
grateful for their advice

00:03:40.541 --> 00:03:42.837
and their concern.

00:03:42.837 --> 00:03:47.213
Because in their caution,
I found my courage.

00:03:47.213 --> 00:03:50.616
In their doubts, I found my resolve.

00:03:50.616 --> 00:03:53.417
And in their warnings, I found my voice

00:03:53.417 --> 00:03:56.220
and chartered my own journey.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.361
And I'm proud of the Vista story.

00:03:58.361 --> 00:04:01.289
We take risks, we do things differently,

00:04:01.289 --> 00:04:03.979
and we listen to our own
voice, we run our own race.

00:04:03.979 --> 00:04:06.005
And it has worked.

00:04:06.005 --> 00:04:07.646
We're now considered to be the number one

00:04:07.646 --> 00:04:10.031
private equity firm on the planet

00:04:10.031 --> 00:04:12.101
and have been so for the last decade.

00:04:12.101 --> 00:04:14.645
- And did you go straight to
Wall Street after college?

00:04:14.645 --> 00:04:16.591
- No, I worked for about six
years in applied research

00:04:16.591 --> 00:04:18.099
and development, you know.

00:04:18.099 --> 00:04:20.948
One of the joys of being
a chemical engineer is,

00:04:20.948 --> 00:04:23.202
you know, we talk about becoming
the modern-day alchemist.

00:04:23.202 --> 00:04:25.762
You learn how to create
things, transform matter

00:04:25.762 --> 00:04:27.360
from one form to another.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.719
And that's what was important
to me, I wanted to be

00:04:29.719 --> 00:04:31.784
an inventor, and so I
worked for about six years

00:04:31.784 --> 00:04:34.515
in applied research
development, got a chance to

00:04:34.515 --> 00:04:37.042
travel all over the world
with big companies like Kraft,

00:04:37.042 --> 00:04:38.993
General Foods, and Air
Products and Chemicals,

00:04:38.993 --> 00:04:41.428
solving some very complex problems.

00:04:41.428 --> 00:04:45.126
And with using my engineering
skillset, and I loved it.

00:04:45.126 --> 00:04:47.462
Absolutely loved it, until one day

00:04:47.462 --> 00:04:50.152
I was sitting there with
the head of my division

00:04:50.152 --> 00:04:53.186
and head of marketing, the head of sales,

00:04:53.186 --> 00:04:55.719
head of development for, you
know, it was a small division,

00:04:55.719 --> 00:04:58.734
but then Philip Morris,
'cause it's part of Kraft.

00:04:58.734 --> 00:05:00.960
And I realized when we went
through our six most important

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.138
strategic priorities, I
was leading five of them.

00:05:04.138 --> 00:05:06.446
I was half the age of
everybody in the room,

00:05:06.446 --> 00:05:08.318
and I know I was making a
third of everybody in the room,

00:05:08.318 --> 00:05:09.423
and I said I'm either
doing something very right

00:05:09.423 --> 00:05:11.296
or very wrong.

00:05:11.296 --> 00:05:13.105
And it was a bit of both, right?

00:05:13.105 --> 00:05:16.500
And so this is kind of that
lesson of the realization

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:18.588
of worth and self-worth,
right, and I said,

00:05:18.588 --> 00:05:20.229
you know what, this is a great experience.

00:05:20.229 --> 00:05:22.140
I'm having fun, I got three promotions

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:23.640
in like 18 months 'cause
I was doing all these

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:25.890
great and wonderful things.

00:05:26.804 --> 00:05:27.912
But then I realized there was probably

00:05:27.912 --> 00:05:29.542
something else out there.

00:05:29.542 --> 00:05:31.311
That you are enough to
be who you want to be

00:05:31.311 --> 00:05:32.899
and to create what you want to create,

00:05:32.899 --> 00:05:36.001
you have to make sure that beyond

00:05:36.001 --> 00:05:37.786
the intention that you put out there,

00:05:37.786 --> 00:05:39.879
you put action to support the intention.

00:05:39.879 --> 00:05:42.700
Don't say I want to be a millionaire,

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:44.624
but don't take the activities to do it.

00:05:44.624 --> 00:05:46.027
Or I want to be the best at something

00:05:46.027 --> 00:05:47.860
but don't practice it.

00:05:49.386 --> 00:05:51.862
It's important to understand
that you are enough pieces,

00:05:51.862 --> 00:05:54.256
you know, put in the
vision, have the vision

00:05:54.256 --> 00:05:56.775
of what you want to become,

00:05:56.775 --> 00:05:59.383
but you have to put consistent
action behind that vision

00:05:59.383 --> 00:06:01.385
in order for that to manifest.

00:06:01.385 --> 00:06:04.308
And it has to be consistent.

00:06:04.308 --> 00:06:05.246
I have the good fortune to be able,

00:06:05.246 --> 00:06:07.700
I was invited here by one
of my fraternity brothers,

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:10.419
Louis Debias, and I remember many a day,

00:06:10.419 --> 00:06:13.679
Louis and I, Friday night,
we're going to go to the party,

00:06:13.679 --> 00:06:15.718
and we went to the party,
and we finished the party

00:06:15.718 --> 00:06:19.801
at 2:30, and three o'clock,
we were back studying

00:06:22.198 --> 00:06:26.031
so that we could do well
on an exam on Monday.

00:06:26.869 --> 00:06:30.285
It is the importance of knowing that

00:06:30.285 --> 00:06:32.632
it's your own intention and
your power and your actions

00:06:32.632 --> 00:06:35.898
that can actually lead to the outcome

00:06:35.898 --> 00:06:38.404
that you want to have manifest.

00:06:38.404 --> 00:06:41.081
And the first and foremost
important thing about

00:06:41.081 --> 00:06:44.248
running your own race is dreaming big.

00:06:45.794 --> 00:06:48.617
And here I'm called back to my childhood

00:06:48.617 --> 00:06:51.351
when I was just starting
elementary school.

00:06:51.351 --> 00:06:54.102
At that point in time, the
Supreme Court had just ruled

00:06:54.102 --> 00:06:57.646
that public school districts
could pursue desegregation

00:06:57.646 --> 00:06:59.855
by using forced busing
to achieve racial balance

00:06:59.855 --> 00:07:01.546
in their schools.

00:07:01.546 --> 00:07:04.071
In fact, I started my own
education as a first grader

00:07:04.071 --> 00:07:06.058
being bused to a school across town,

00:07:06.058 --> 00:07:07.709
even though my father was a principal

00:07:07.709 --> 00:07:10.931
at a school five blocks away.

00:07:10.931 --> 00:07:13.415
Although I was a live subject
in one of the nation's

00:07:13.415 --> 00:07:16.081
most controversial legal
debates, I frankly didn't know

00:07:16.081 --> 00:07:18.478
what the big deal was all about 'cause

00:07:18.478 --> 00:07:22.172
these kids who didn't look like
me sure acted just like me.

00:07:22.172 --> 00:07:24.932
But desegregation was a
big deal to my parents,

00:07:24.932 --> 00:07:28.547
and leaders of their generations
knew that great change

00:07:28.547 --> 00:07:31.495
was in store, and it was
the right thing to do.

00:07:31.495 --> 00:07:34.326
And they often met with resistance,

00:07:34.326 --> 00:07:37.465
and often violent resistance.

00:07:37.465 --> 00:07:40.541
But because of their
struggle, their sacrifice,

00:07:40.541 --> 00:07:42.541
I received a great gift.

00:07:43.977 --> 00:07:46.727
And that's the gift to dream big.

00:07:47.873 --> 00:07:51.155
I knew my history, I remembered
the pride of my mother

00:07:51.155 --> 00:07:53.593
telling about when she
took me and my brother,

00:07:53.593 --> 00:07:55.705
brought us back to Washington, D.C.

00:07:55.705 --> 00:07:57.753
and held me as a nine-month-old

00:07:57.753 --> 00:08:00.174
during the March on Washington
when Martin Luther King

00:08:00.174 --> 00:08:02.753
laid out his dream for
equal and harmonious

00:08:02.753 --> 00:08:04.503
meritocratic America.

00:08:05.681 --> 00:08:08.399
And dreaming big to me
meant knowing my history,

00:08:08.399 --> 00:08:10.566
but not being bound by it.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:15.004
It meant harnessing the past
to drive me into the future.

00:08:15.004 --> 00:08:17.477
It meant grounding myself in who I was

00:08:17.477 --> 00:08:19.227
and where I came from

00:08:20.097 --> 00:08:23.336
so I could soar to become
who I wanted to become.

00:08:23.336 --> 00:08:26.419
The sponsors of Robert Smith's career

00:08:28.127 --> 00:08:32.301
only did so after I demonstrated
that I'm willing to make

00:08:32.301 --> 00:08:34.634
the commitment to my career.

00:08:35.511 --> 00:08:37.722
It's a two-way street,
it's not just, oh, here's,

00:08:37.722 --> 00:08:40.173
you know, the person
you're going to sponsor.

00:08:40.173 --> 00:08:42.140
Make sure that they get on good deals.

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:44.262
No, it is, they see you in the office,

00:08:44.262 --> 00:08:45.635
you're grinding a hundred hours,

00:08:45.635 --> 00:08:47.515
you're getting it done, you're making sure

00:08:47.515 --> 00:08:48.856
there's no mistakes, and
if there is a mistake,

00:08:48.856 --> 00:08:49.924
you correct it.

00:08:49.924 --> 00:08:51.521
You're following up,
you're reaching outside

00:08:51.521 --> 00:08:54.898
of your knowledge to go learn more.

00:08:54.898 --> 00:08:56.824
When you do that, sponsors
are more than happy

00:08:56.824 --> 00:08:59.157
to then take credit for you.

00:09:00.123 --> 00:09:02.314
Okay, and that has been my experience,

00:09:02.314 --> 00:09:03.749
but it has all come through

00:09:03.749 --> 00:09:08.146
you got to demonstrate
that that's who you are

00:09:08.146 --> 00:09:09.580
and that's your priority.

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:11.345
If it's Friday night and it's five o'clock

00:09:11.345 --> 00:09:13.644
and your sponsor seeing
you leaving 'cause you got,

00:09:13.644 --> 00:09:16.233
you know, happy hour with your boys,

00:09:16.233 --> 00:09:18.480
well, guess what, and I
mean, and they're there.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:22.164
You got to be conscious of
that sort of stuff, okay?

00:09:22.164 --> 00:09:24.107
I like when they come back in on Saturday,

00:09:24.107 --> 00:09:25.088
'cause they did come back, and I'm wearing

00:09:25.088 --> 00:09:27.134
the same thing I wore
on Friday night, right?

00:09:27.134 --> 00:09:29.488
Okay, this guy's dedicated to his craft.

00:09:29.488 --> 00:09:32.505
And so when this deal happens,
they're happy to stand up

00:09:32.505 --> 00:09:35.169
in front of the managing
principal of the firm

00:09:35.169 --> 00:09:38.183
at that time to say, hey,
Robert did this work.

00:09:38.183 --> 00:09:39.223
You see what I mean?

00:09:39.223 --> 00:09:41.068
So it's a two-way street.

00:09:41.068 --> 00:09:43.708
And sponsors, you know,
again, I have the opportunity,

00:09:43.708 --> 00:09:46.439
and I do in my firm,
build and develop people.

00:09:46.439 --> 00:09:48.295
But we have, when we do our reviews,

00:09:48.295 --> 00:09:49.763
part of what we're looking
at, do you have that

00:09:49.763 --> 00:09:51.357
kind of grit, are you dedicated to this?

00:09:51.357 --> 00:09:54.107
This is not a lifestyle business.

00:09:55.136 --> 00:09:56.635
And if you want to make
it a lifestyle business,

00:09:56.635 --> 00:09:59.108
this is the wrong business to be in, okay?

00:09:59.108 --> 00:10:02.437
So that's, in the world I
live in, that's something

00:10:02.437 --> 00:10:05.342
we think about and we talk
about in that context.

00:10:05.342 --> 00:10:06.682
That's just the nature of it.

00:10:06.682 --> 00:10:09.820
So we look for, you know,
again, I will sponsor those,

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:11.990
any of my folks now who
have not grown up with me

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:16.377
in this business, I sponsor
them because I saw them

00:10:16.377 --> 00:10:19.218
putting that level of effort

00:10:19.218 --> 00:10:21.523
and energy in 15 years
ago, and guess what,

00:10:21.523 --> 00:10:23.114
they still do it today.

00:10:23.114 --> 00:10:26.160
In America, education is not enough.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:28.894
Especially for us as African Americans.

00:10:28.894 --> 00:10:33.180
You also need to have
access to the economy.

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:37.171
And so as I'm now going
from elementary school

00:10:37.171 --> 00:10:39.897
to middle school to high
school, I started to actually

00:10:39.897 --> 00:10:42.215
think about, well, what
is access to the economy?

00:10:42.215 --> 00:10:45.010
Why do they live in
that great house and, A,

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:47.301
their father is a contracting, but owns

00:10:47.301 --> 00:10:49.718
his own contracting business.

00:10:51.125 --> 00:10:52.839
And yet, down the street, I had a friend,

00:10:52.839 --> 00:10:56.052
his father worked for a
contractor, but didn't own

00:10:56.052 --> 00:10:59.061
his own contracting business,
and he lived near us.

00:10:59.061 --> 00:11:01.393
And when I looked at
that, I said, you know,

00:11:01.393 --> 00:11:03.842
part of what has to
happen, and in my mind,

00:11:03.842 --> 00:11:06.896
was I needed to, and I wanted
to become a business owner.

00:11:06.896 --> 00:11:09.446
And that was early on,
so literally, you know,

00:11:09.446 --> 00:11:11.467
I started my first business
when I was probably

00:11:11.467 --> 00:11:13.236
nine or 10 years old, I
was selling golf balls,

00:11:13.236 --> 00:11:15.659
and I cut grass, I did
all those sorts of things,

00:11:15.659 --> 00:11:18.020
'cause the fact of the matter
is, I actually didn't like

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:20.917
wearing my brother's clothes
until I was 14 years old.

00:11:20.917 --> 00:11:22.381
You know, I wore hand-me-downs basically

00:11:22.381 --> 00:11:23.798
until that period of time.

00:11:23.798 --> 00:11:25.630
And I don't know if any of
you have experienced that,

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:27.827
but, you know, kids can
be cruel in some respects.

00:11:27.827 --> 00:11:30.904
But part of that also hardens
you to help you understand

00:11:30.904 --> 00:11:33.128
and help you to reach some of your goals,

00:11:33.128 --> 00:11:36.211
and part of my goal was to be part of

00:11:37.078 --> 00:11:38.962
an economic class in
America where I could buy

00:11:38.962 --> 00:11:41.341
my own clothes, the ones I wanted,

00:11:41.341 --> 00:11:43.227
and buy the shoes that I wanted,

00:11:43.227 --> 00:11:45.204
and go the places I wanted.

00:11:45.204 --> 00:11:49.971
And one of the advantages
of, again, having parents who

00:11:49.971 --> 00:11:51.770
wanted me to read and forced me to read,

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:54.599
I got a chance to read
a number of things, and

00:11:54.599 --> 00:11:56.349
one of the things that
inspired me early in my life

00:11:56.349 --> 00:11:59.052
was actually, believe it or
not, the James Bond novels.

00:11:59.052 --> 00:12:02.530
Because as I read them, they
took me and transported me

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:06.830
to places that I had no idea
actually really existed.

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:09.019
And if you ever seen any
of those movies, I mean,

00:12:09.019 --> 00:12:10.512
they characterize him in
these movies, and I was like,

00:12:10.512 --> 00:12:12.072
wow, this is pretty terrific.

00:12:12.072 --> 00:12:15.267
I want to figure out a way to
actually get to that place.

00:12:15.267 --> 00:12:19.742
And that is part of the
impetus behind me working hard

00:12:19.742 --> 00:12:22.637
and also driving myself to go figure out

00:12:22.637 --> 00:12:25.139
how to create an opportunity

00:12:25.139 --> 00:12:27.490
that I could actually make big and lasting

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:29.364
and have an impact.

00:12:29.364 --> 00:12:32.236
So when I got to Bell
Labs, they shared offices,

00:12:32.236 --> 00:12:34.994
and I happened to share offices with a PhD

00:12:34.994 --> 00:12:36.927
in chemical engineering.

00:12:36.927 --> 00:12:40.986
And this man had patents that
were probably 30 pages long.

00:12:40.986 --> 00:12:43.216
And he had become one of my first mentors.

00:12:43.216 --> 00:12:45.250
And on my first day of
work, as we got settled

00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:48.159
into his office, he
turned his chair around,

00:12:48.159 --> 00:12:50.132
he kind of looked me up
and down and held this

00:12:50.132 --> 00:12:52.658
computer chip in front of me and said,

00:12:52.658 --> 00:12:54.601
"This is an operational amplifier.

00:12:54.601 --> 00:12:57.473
"It's failing in our Merlin
systems out there in the field.

00:12:57.473 --> 00:12:59.462
"You need to figure out why it's failing

00:12:59.462 --> 00:13:00.786
"so we can fix it.

00:13:00.786 --> 00:13:03.433
"If you have any questions, ask me."

00:13:03.433 --> 00:13:05.516
And then he turns around.

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:09.231
And so you see, unlike today's
technically sophisticated

00:13:09.231 --> 00:13:10.917
high school students, I had no idea

00:13:10.917 --> 00:13:13.688
what an operational amplifier was,

00:13:13.688 --> 00:13:16.719
what it was supposed to do,
what this one didn't do.

00:13:16.719 --> 00:13:20.013
And so before the days
of Google and Wikipedia,

00:13:20.013 --> 00:13:23.282
we had this thing called the library.

00:13:23.282 --> 00:13:27.179
And I went to the library
and I pulled every book

00:13:27.179 --> 00:13:29.366
on operational amplifiers I could find.

00:13:29.366 --> 00:13:32.294
I talked to everyone in
the halls who would listen

00:13:32.294 --> 00:13:33.961
and asked questions.

00:13:35.249 --> 00:13:38.239
And by the end of the summer,
not only did I have an idea

00:13:38.239 --> 00:13:41.289
about why this wasn't working,
but I had built a system

00:13:41.289 --> 00:13:43.805
that simulates the field conditions

00:13:43.805 --> 00:13:45.766
that causes them to fail.

00:13:45.766 --> 00:13:49.881
And then with my mentor's
help, we fixed the problem.

00:13:49.881 --> 00:13:54.656
My guess is even now, I'm
probably the most knowledgeable

00:13:54.656 --> 00:13:57.716
intern at Bell Labs on
operational amplifiers

00:13:57.716 --> 00:14:00.552
in the history of that company.

00:14:00.552 --> 00:14:03.417
But the important thing was
the challenge from my mentor

00:14:03.417 --> 00:14:05.752
was more than to teach
me something about this

00:14:05.752 --> 00:14:08.576
obscure integrated circuit.

00:14:08.576 --> 00:14:10.947
It's a challenge that has
reaped rich dividends for me

00:14:10.947 --> 00:14:12.780
over my entire career.

00:14:14.255 --> 00:14:17.356
It is the joy of figuring things out.

00:14:17.356 --> 00:14:19.826
- What do you think the
accelerators to success are,

00:14:19.826 --> 00:14:22.433
particularly in environments
where structures

00:14:22.433 --> 00:14:24.588
are not designed to help people succeed?

00:14:24.588 --> 00:14:25.421
- Sure.

00:14:27.873 --> 00:14:29.206
This is complex.

00:14:31.009 --> 00:14:33.843
There's an education piece.

00:14:33.843 --> 00:14:36.235
Then there's a knowledge piece.

00:14:36.235 --> 00:14:38.916
And then there's a
practitioner of a craft piece

00:14:38.916 --> 00:14:43.434
that all are important, and
you do need to have guides

00:14:43.434 --> 00:14:44.601
along the way.

00:14:46.515 --> 00:14:50.098
At least if you're a
person of color, okay?

00:14:51.044 --> 00:14:53.756
And I say that because,

00:14:53.756 --> 00:14:55.694
you know, being a person of
color, I see the difference

00:14:55.694 --> 00:14:58.778
in my experiences versus others

00:14:58.778 --> 00:15:01.999
in what their experiences have been.

00:15:01.999 --> 00:15:04.499
And I wouldn't say that it is,

00:15:05.673 --> 00:15:07.358
who knows it's overt or
not, I just can tell you

00:15:07.358 --> 00:15:08.769
what my experience has been.

00:15:08.769 --> 00:15:12.832
So first of all, you
have to do well enough,

00:15:12.832 --> 00:15:13.665
no, actually not.

00:15:13.665 --> 00:15:16.748
You have to have a desire to be great

00:15:18.123 --> 00:15:19.694
at what is you want to do, first of all,

00:15:19.694 --> 00:15:22.863
to do this business,
that's the first thing.

00:15:22.863 --> 00:15:26.475
There's a fundamental base
level of understanding of,

00:15:26.475 --> 00:15:29.011
you know, basic math.

00:15:29.011 --> 00:15:32.682
You have to be good at it,
okay, really good at it.

00:15:32.682 --> 00:15:35.183
And then from there you have to understand

00:15:35.183 --> 00:15:37.399
and start to listen to the structures

00:15:37.399 --> 00:15:40.398
around how deals are
done, and there's a whole

00:15:40.398 --> 00:15:42.912
fact pattern capturing that's important,

00:15:42.912 --> 00:15:45.412
that while you can listen, the only way

00:15:45.412 --> 00:15:47.378
to really learn it is to do it.

00:15:47.378 --> 00:15:50.300
And you have to become expert,
and there are talks about,

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:51.370
you know, what is an expert?

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:53.034
Well, an expert's someone who dedicates

00:15:53.034 --> 00:15:56.063
10,000 hours to their craft.

00:15:56.063 --> 00:15:57.753
Okay, that's a lot of time.

00:15:57.753 --> 00:15:59.817
Not over 20 years, but
in as compressed a period

00:15:59.817 --> 00:16:01.101
of time as possible.

00:16:01.101 --> 00:16:03.136
The advantage of going
to these investment banks

00:16:03.136 --> 00:16:07.114
is in a heady environment,
that happens, you know,

00:16:07.114 --> 00:16:08.504
in hundred hour work weeks.

00:16:08.504 --> 00:16:09.337
- [Woman] Right.

00:16:09.337 --> 00:16:12.934
- Right, and, you know,
while all your friends

00:16:12.934 --> 00:16:14.656
who you graduate college
with are going out

00:16:14.656 --> 00:16:15.938
on Friday night and Saturday night,

00:16:15.938 --> 00:16:18.949
guess what, you're
working, you're working,

00:16:18.949 --> 00:16:20.756
you are working.

00:16:20.756 --> 00:16:23.839
But that's how you build that fabric.

00:16:27.618 --> 00:16:30.644
To be an expert at your
craft, you cannot do

00:16:30.644 --> 00:16:33.901
this business well if you are
not an expert at your craft.

00:16:33.901 --> 00:16:36.382
So all of those things, first
of all, you have to have

00:16:36.382 --> 00:16:38.716
structures and opportunity to see it.

00:16:38.716 --> 00:16:40.584
Then you have to dedicate yourself to it.

00:16:40.584 --> 00:16:42.646
And then you have to have
people who can help you

00:16:42.646 --> 00:16:45.813
understand and navigate to some extent

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:50.028
why certain deals went one
way and why certain deals

00:16:50.028 --> 00:16:54.317
went the other way, and
it's highly nuanced.

00:16:54.317 --> 00:16:58.484
It isn't something that you
can just read in a case study.

00:17:00.051 --> 00:17:02.876
You have to live it,
and you have to live it

00:17:02.876 --> 00:17:05.348
through the eyes of
someone who has lived it.

00:17:05.348 --> 00:17:08.745
And to the extent you see
people who have lived it

00:17:08.745 --> 00:17:12.814
and you're in that environment,
you have to now seek out

00:17:12.814 --> 00:17:16.691
the time and force that
engagement of why it went

00:17:16.691 --> 00:17:19.039
one way versus another.

00:17:19.039 --> 00:17:23.502
And see how that fits in the
context of the deal business.

00:17:23.502 --> 00:17:26.934
So those are I mean,
it's complex, and it is.

00:17:26.934 --> 00:17:28.267
But the rewards,

00:17:29.354 --> 00:17:31.299
beyond the personal
rewards, but, you know,

00:17:31.299 --> 00:17:34.466
the rewards are just astounding, okay,

00:17:35.937 --> 00:17:37.996
in terms of personal growth,
personal development,

00:17:37.996 --> 00:17:40.293
and frankly, the opportunity to then train

00:17:40.293 --> 00:17:43.351
and build others so that
they can become experts

00:17:43.351 --> 00:17:45.169
in their craft and realize
their dreams, right?

00:17:45.169 --> 00:17:49.954
So I think that's the arc
of the opportunity there,

00:17:49.954 --> 00:17:54.732
that is in this world, I
mean, it's a rarefied era,

00:17:54.732 --> 00:17:56.053
people who are really good at this

00:17:56.053 --> 00:17:57.526
and who really get an opportunity to do it

00:17:57.526 --> 00:18:02.105
and practice it at a size
and scale that matters.

00:18:02.105 --> 00:18:04.196
You know, in this world,

00:18:04.196 --> 00:18:06.982
the importance of actually
going through the process

00:18:06.982 --> 00:18:09.407
of understanding what the problem is

00:18:09.407 --> 00:18:10.905
and then figuring it out yourself.

00:18:10.905 --> 00:18:12.746
And every now and then you
need some help, that's fine.

00:18:12.746 --> 00:18:14.495
But if you get too much
help, you never figure out

00:18:14.495 --> 00:18:18.741
how to do things, and you
don't develop the grit.

00:18:18.741 --> 00:18:20.334
And some of the older
folks in this audience

00:18:20.334 --> 00:18:22.309
are shaking their head,
yeah, you got to have grit,

00:18:22.309 --> 00:18:24.235
and grit means getting
turned away from things

00:18:24.235 --> 00:18:27.293
14, 15 times, calling
someone every two weeks,

00:18:27.293 --> 00:18:29.723
you know, every day for five months,

00:18:29.723 --> 00:18:31.613
and then finally it materializing

00:18:31.613 --> 00:18:34.279
in something that you want.

00:18:34.279 --> 00:18:38.697
And discovering the joy of
figuring things out means

00:18:38.697 --> 00:18:40.392
fight through those problems.

00:18:40.392 --> 00:18:42.628
Understand and learn how
you address those problems

00:18:42.628 --> 00:18:46.432
and come up with the
answers to those problems

00:18:46.432 --> 00:18:50.221
and a solution that is uniquely yours.

00:18:50.221 --> 00:18:53.084
That's an important part of
growing up and solving problems,

00:18:53.084 --> 00:18:55.247
'cause if you can't
solve easy problems now,

00:18:55.247 --> 00:18:59.414
you will not be able to solve
difficult problems later.

00:19:00.418 --> 00:19:02.026
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:19:02.026 --> 00:19:05.207
I made this video because
Arlethia White asked me to.

00:19:05.207 --> 00:19:06.846
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:19:06.846 --> 00:19:09.078
to profile next, leave
it in the comments below,

00:19:09.078 --> 00:19:10.123
and I'll see what I can do.

00:19:10.123 --> 00:19:12.638
I'd also love to know
what of Robert's message

00:19:12.638 --> 00:19:14.658
had the biggest impact on you and why.

00:19:14.658 --> 00:19:16.956
What was your favorite clip,
what did you learn from this,

00:19:16.956 --> 00:19:18.967
how is it going to impact
your life or your business?

00:19:18.967 --> 00:19:20.678
Please leave it in the comments below.

00:19:20.678 --> 00:19:22.378
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:19:22.378 --> 00:19:23.475
I also want to give a quick shout out

00:19:23.475 --> 00:19:26.666
to Jason "J-Ryze" Fonceca
from ryzeonline.com.

00:19:26.666 --> 00:19:28.422
Thank you, J-Ryze, for
picking up another copy

00:19:28.422 --> 00:19:31.214
of my book, it really, really,
really means a lot to me.

00:19:31.214 --> 00:19:32.749
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:19:32.749 --> 00:19:35.485
I believe in you, I hope
you continue to believe

00:19:35.485 --> 00:19:38.827
in yourself and whatever your one word is.

00:19:38.827 --> 00:19:41.249
Much love, I'll see you seen.

00:19:41.249 --> 00:19:43.582
(whooshing)

00:19:47.269 --> 00:19:49.651
- I went to the same high
school in East Denver

00:19:49.651 --> 00:19:51.949
as my father did, Denver East High School,

00:19:51.949 --> 00:19:54.651
and I remember taking a
computer science course.

00:19:54.651 --> 00:19:57.407
And at the time, they
introduced this thing called

00:19:57.407 --> 00:19:59.755
a transistor, which is the
building block of computers

00:19:59.755 --> 00:20:02.027
for those of you who don't know.

00:20:02.027 --> 00:20:03.955
And transistors were
invented at this place called

00:20:03.955 --> 00:20:08.122
Bell Labs, which had a facility
about 20 miles from my home.

00:20:09.191 --> 00:20:11.166
And after that lesson, I went and I dug up

00:20:11.166 --> 00:20:13.351
the telephone number to
the Bell Labs facility,

00:20:13.351 --> 00:20:16.269
and I called 'em, I said, "Do
you have summer internships?"

00:20:16.269 --> 00:20:17.770
They said, "We absolutely do."

00:20:17.770 --> 00:20:19.437
I said, "Fantastic."

00:20:20.447 --> 00:20:22.039
They said, "If you're between your junior

00:20:22.039 --> 00:20:23.146
"and senior year in college."

00:20:23.146 --> 00:20:24.765
I said, "Hey, I'm a junior in high school,

00:20:24.765 --> 00:20:27.399
"I'm taking advanced math
classes and computer science,

00:20:27.399 --> 00:20:29.857
"getting all A's, just
like being in college."

00:20:29.857 --> 00:20:32.116
(audience laughing)

00:20:32.116 --> 00:20:33.441
They said, "No, it isn't."

00:20:33.441 --> 00:20:35.855
(audience laughing)

00:20:35.855 --> 00:20:37.605
So I called every day

00:20:39.562 --> 00:20:41.824
for two weeks straight.

00:20:41.824 --> 00:20:43.859
The HR director of course
stopped taking the calls

00:20:43.859 --> 00:20:45.609
after the second day.

00:20:46.464 --> 00:20:49.846
And I left a message with my phone number.

00:20:49.846 --> 00:20:53.429
Then I called every
Monday for five months.

00:20:55.512 --> 00:20:58.877
And every time I called, the
receptionist just chuckled,

00:20:58.877 --> 00:21:01.341
and she took my name,
and she took my number,

00:21:01.341 --> 00:21:03.102
and she said, "We'll get back to you

00:21:03.102 --> 00:21:04.952
"if there's an opening."

00:21:04.952 --> 00:21:06.550
And I kept at it.

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:09.614
And to my surprise, the
human resources director

00:21:09.614 --> 00:21:11.531
called my house in June

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:16.571
and said, "Listen, an intern
from MIT didn't show up.

00:21:16.571 --> 00:21:19.510
"We have an extra slot, we're
not promising you anything,

00:21:19.510 --> 00:21:21.701
"but why don't you come
on in and interview."

00:21:21.701 --> 00:21:23.603
Now of course I knew I
was the most qualified

00:21:23.603 --> 00:21:24.958
candidate for this job.

00:21:24.958 --> 00:21:27.421
(audience laughing)

00:21:27.421 --> 00:21:29.754
And the truth is, I went in,

00:21:30.712 --> 00:21:32.649
I interviewed, I got the job.

00:21:32.649 --> 00:21:35.938
So the important thing that I
want you to think about here

00:21:35.938 --> 00:21:38.532
is the persistence to get that job

00:21:38.532 --> 00:21:40.871
led to me working at Bell Labs

00:21:40.871 --> 00:21:43.018
for the next four years,

00:21:43.018 --> 00:21:45.940
becoming a co-op student,
and ultimately finishing

00:21:45.940 --> 00:21:48.822
with a degree in chemical
engineering from Cornell,

00:21:48.822 --> 00:21:50.437
all from being persistent.

